main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ absent operator- => false: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ present operator- => true: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ int => true: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
--
Currently: assertions 2 and 3 FAIL -- the return-type-requirement of a
compound requirement is never satisfied.
Round-90 diagnosis (TWO stacked defects, both located):
1. [temp.constr.atomic]/1: the concept's normalised body must be
   CONTEXTUALLY CONVERTED to bool.  A concept over a trait member
   (`static const bool value`) folds to a c_bool constant, which
   exprt::is_true() does not recognise, so every SATISFIED
   return-type-requirement read as unsatisfied.  Converting the
   constant (as cpp_typecheck_resolve.cpp's tri-state evaluator already
   does for requires-clauses) fixes assertion 3.
2. With that applied, assertion 2 (class with a MEMBER operator-) still
   fails.  ROUND-90 ROOT (sharper than "specialization selection"): the
   STORED concept body's template argument is an `ambiguous` node with an
   EMPTY type -- the reference to the concept's own parameter is not
   recoverable from the body at all, so neither template_mapt::apply nor
   a by-name walk can substitute it (an added walk over `template_args`
   arguments changes nothing: there is no name to match).  The dumped
   pre-typecheck body is BYTE-IDENTICAL for the satisfied and the
   unsatisfied case; the verdict therefore comes from ambient
   template_map/scope state when `is_int_<...>` is resolved, which is why
   the same body yields different answers for different Es (plain `int`
   -> primary/false, `const int` -> specialization/true, both wrong for
   the wrong reason).  The fix must make the concept's parameter
   RECOVERABLE in the stored body (or evaluate the concept through the
   normal instantiation path with explicit arguments) rather than patch
   the substitution.
   Verified separately: `is_int_<int>`, `is_int_<decltype(a-b)>` and
   `is_int_<decltype((a-b))>` all resolve CORRECTLY outside a
   requires-expression, so ordinary specialization matching is fine.
DO NOT land fix 1 alone: it makes previously-(wrongly)-unsatisfied
requirements satisfied and regressed 18 cbmc-cpp tests
(cpp20_vector_*, cpp20_span*, cpp20_ranges_basic, cpp20_erase_if*,
iterator_traits/concept chains) -- the surrounding C++20 library
machinery currently depends on the wrong answer.  Both layers must land
together, ideally with fix 2 first.
